  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1031 PM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nAs the monsoon high shifts over the southern Plains this weekend, \ndrier air will filter in from the east. PWs fall to about 1\" for the \nlowlands on Sat and closer to 0.9\" on Sun. Mainly dry conditions are \nforecast both days with low-medium storm chances favoring area mtns \nand west of the Divide (moisture plume concentrated over E AZ). \nBrief downpours and gusty winds are the primary threats as \nprevailing winds will be light from the S-SE.\n\nGlobal ensemble means still show the upper high stretching from the \nsouthern Plains to the Carolinas from Mon onward with troughing over \nthe West Coast. A modest increase in storm coverage is forecast for \nMon as PWs rise to about 1.1\" with slight rain chances in the \nlowlands. Western areas will again be favored for storms on Mon as \nthe moisture plume inches eastward. Models are showing the upper \nhigh focusing over the Carolinas/TN Valley into midweek, weakening \nthe moisture plume in our region and providing little if any \nforcing. Low mtn storm chances are forecast Tue-Thu with mainly dry \nconditions for the lowlands. The high then looks to centralize over \nthe southern Plains again late in the week and reinvigorate the \nmoisture plume a bit, but there are more significant model\ndifferences. Areas to the north and west appear best set up for \nstorm chances to end the period. \n\nTemps will be steady through next week with highs 5-10 degrees above \nnormal. Lowland highs of 95-100F are forecast each day. Winds remain \nlight through midweek, shifting more W by Mon.",
